# A gate must assert its own subject exists

**Absence of signal is not evidence of absence of defect. It is, first,
evidence that the subject may be absent.**

A gate that examines nothing reports the same green as a gate that
examines everything and finds nothing wrong. Every instance below
computed a *correct* answer about a surface that was not there, and
reported it as a clean signal.

## Where this has already bitten

Promoted from the RandomX v2 T18 mutation-regime round
([`docs/design/RANDOMX_V2_MUTATION_REGIME.md`](../../docs/design/RANDOMX_V2_MUTATION_REGIME.md)
§13), which found six instances of the shape and three more in its own
proposed fixes:

| The surface that wasn't there | How it read |
| --- | --- |
| A job whose YAML header was severed by an edit | weekly cron green |
| A config file at a path the tool never reads | discipline "enforced" |
| Test scoping that excluded the judging crate | mutants "caught" |
| An `assert_eq!` — macros are not mutation sites | survivor count clean |
| A branch no test could reach | tests "passing" |
| An `if:` whose operator precedence widened the trigger | job "gated" |

The fixes are the tell. Three separate proposals shared the defect's
shape: a setting written into a file nothing reads, an exclusion for a
class that cannot occur, and a name-matched scope that would go green
over an empty set. None was caught by noticing it was wrong on its own
terms. Each was caught by asking **"does the thing this acts on
exist?"** — which is cheap, mechanical, and was not part of the process
until it had cost five sessions.

## The rule

When adding or reviewing a gate, ask what a green result would look like
if its subject vanished. If green and vacuous are indistinguishable, the
gate needs an assertion that its subject is present, and that assertion
must fail loudly.

Concrete forms already in this repo — these are instances of one rule,
not separate tricks:

- **Assert the population is non-empty.** A name- or path-filtered
  selection (`--re`, `--file`, a ctest label) that matches nothing runs
  clean. Pin a minimum count; `ctest --no-tests=error` is the same idea.
- **Assert an exclusion excludes something.** A skip-list entry that
  matches no real path is a silent no-op, and the next entry written in
  the same style will be too.
- **Assert the prerequisite is present**, rather than installing or
  assuming it — and prefer verification to installation when the tool
  is already there, so the check cannot be taken out by an unrelated
  network failure.
- **Read code, not prose.** A gate that greps un-stripped source can be
  held green by a doc comment quoting the very literal whose definition
  moved (the SA-3b domain-registry gate strips comments for exactly
  this reason).
- **Keep the verdict out of a pipe** — see
  [`46-shell-gate-exits`](46-shell-gate-exits.mdc). A swallowed exit
  status is this same failure at the last step: the gate ran, judged,
  and the judgement never reached anyone.

## Scope, and what this is not

This is about a gate's *subject*, not its *strictness*. A gate that
examines the right surface and is merely lenient is a coverage question,
argued on its own merits. A gate that examines nothing is not lenient —
it is absent, while appearing present, which is worse than having no
gate at all: a missing gate is visible, and this one is not.

The corollary for deletions: before removing a job, a trigger, or a
config, check who else consumes it. The T18 round nearly deleted a cron
that looked like one job's private trigger and was in fact what selected
another job's full sweep.
